</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Family units treated as micro-enterprises, often with one dominant wage earner or head of household.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Gendered labor division enforced individual responsibility over communal support, embedding stress into household fields.</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="3">
<li class="break-words">De Agricultura (Pliny, Roman era):</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Early prescriptions for household management emphasized self-sufficiency, management of labor, and maximizing land yield.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Codified the idea that success or failure of a household depends on strategic mastery of resources, not communal stability.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">This sets a longitudinal field of cultural expectation: each household = local Œ¶ spike of productivity.</p>
